> plain text, or hex-encoded binaries. All those protocols are standards.

But only the Usenet protocols are designed for discussions. Of course
HTTP doesn't help you there, that's not what it was developed for.

> While Usenet carries mostly unstructured (i.e. non-standardized) plain
> text, 

That's not true. You've got the message headers where stuff like
posting date, poster, user agent, message-id, thread information etc.
is encoded in a standard, easily machine-parsable way.

The message body is less standardized but there are some widely used
practices for text messages. For example > as quoting characters,
"-- \n" as a signature separator or the "replied_to_poster wrote:"
line.

All this is needed to build up groups, threads, replies or new topics
or for improving the display single messages.

Of course all that information is available to the web forum server,
too. But it isn't exported in a usable(==machine-parseable) way to the
browser.

> the "Web" carries data at least in a standardized format; not that
> this matters, though, since in both cases there's no real operational
> semantics defined for that data[*] - which are of course an issue of the
> underlying protocols. [*](In case of HTML it's only presentation hints.)
>
> The problem with Web clients for Usenet access is that they are re-using
> a method designed for some sort of application inappropriately for some
> completely different task.

I didn't even consider that. It's unfortunate that web interfaces that
NNTP web interfaces are usually even worse than standard web forums
(yes, Google Groups, I'm talking about you).

> Or, said from a different perspective; the
> Web (and it's protocols and extensions) is nowadays used for everything.

That's not a problem in itself. You can build new standards,
protocols and extensions on that existing infrastructure. For example
RSS builds upon XML or microformats are extending normal HTML
documents in an inobtrusive way.

But I was saying that there is *no* standard for the purposes of
discussion forums (yet). I can't direct my browser to a web forum and
get any support for reading the messages there that go beyond what the
web server software offers.

You only get the messages and threads as unstructured HTML, intended
only for displaying in a browser, not structured data for displaying
in a client supporting the protocol.

Just a simple example. Every Usenet client can be considered broken if
it doesn't include a "ignore this poster" function. Not so web forums.
Being able to ignore specific users is a feature not commonly
available, let alone "advanced" stuff like scoring.

If there were a standard for discussion platforms on the net, you
could have a plugin that automatically hides messages from a specific
user or even the browser could have support for that itself (like many
browsers have nowadays builtin support for RSS).

> Though the original thesis was that Usenet would be replaced by Web
> forums. Probably. In case the new generations get to learn only the
> contemporary inventions all ancient pearls will get forgotten and lost.

For most things this has already happened. Usenet might never go away
completely, after all there are still Gopher and BBS sites around. But
it certainly won't ever recover from web forums. But one can hope that
web forums get replaced or are transformed into something that's
better than web forums (easy) and also better than Usenet (not so
easy).

